  "body": "Follow-up to #2832. It proves that conjecture K3 holds. This is a research note: no \u03ba claim, no supplier word, no change to any existing file. Only `research/two-sided-ten-term-floor/` is added.\n\n## Result\n\n**Theorem K3.** Every normalised two-sided decoder of `N\u2080 = G/2` on P112 (dense h = 9 table) with `m = 10` terms and `(s, t) = (1, 1)` costs at least 64. Normalised means no two proportional `p`'s or `q`'s (Lemma D of #2338). The decoder may have `J_P \u2229 J_Q` disjoint or not.\n\n- **Sharp.** Two exact decoders in the class cost exactly 64 (`P Q\u1d40 = N\u2080` on all 112\u00b2 entries, no proportional terms): one with `J_P \u2229 J_Q = \u2205` (`|J_P| = |J_Q| = 3`) and one with `|J_P \u2229 J_Q| = 2`.\n- **Proof.** The bound is `cost = 90 \u2212 \u03a3 s_k` with the exact identity `cost = 72 \u2212 D\u0302_P \u2212 D\u0302_Q + \u03b6_P + \u03b6_Q + \u039b`, where `D\u0302` summarises the low-weight outside rows and columns (one low of deficiency `D \u2265 3`, one or two weight-7 lows, or none). Every `(D\u0302_P, D\u0302_Q)` cell is closed:\n  - sum \u2264 8: the identity itself.\n  - a side with `D\u0302 \u2264 3`: the other side needs `D\u0302 \u2265 6`, so at least five lossless frame-row dual terms with outside columns in non-frame classes; the pair systems have no 5-clique. This is exact on all 93 sets `I^c` with `|I^c| \u2264 3`; the independent `verify_cl.py` gives largest cliques 2, 2, 2, 4.\n  - both sides `\u2265 4`: `\u03a3 s \u2264 26` when the two lows sit in different terms (swap slots are `\u03a9`-orthonormal, and each extra saving costs a slot). If one term is low on both sides, `\u03a3 s \u2264 36 \u2212 cost_b \u2212 (D_P + D_Q)`; the equality case (`cost_b = 0`, `D_P + D_Q = 9`) is impossible. One written proof covers it, with idempotents `A + B = I\u2088` for repeated indices and the Steiner system S(3,4,8) for distinct ones.\n- **Dependencies.** Conditional on the open PR #2816 only (#1212, #2724 and #2826 are not used) (Lemma 3.1, cover facts C1/C3, `g(5) = 12`, completeness of the direction list), through #2832 Lemmas 2.3 and 3.7\u20133.9, Theorem 3.5, Corollary 3.6. #2724 is not used, and neither is any disjointness hypothesis (the disjoint sub-case is covered again; #2832 Theorem 3.13 is an independent proof of it).\n\n## Consequence for #2832\n\nK3 is now a theorem, conditional as stated. With #2832, the P112 master `C(P112) \u2265 64` reduces to **K1** (every two-sided decoder of `N\u2080(P112)`, general `m, s, t`) and **K2** (excess pays for corrections, one-sided) only. Nothing is claimed about K1, K2, P120 or any \u03ba.\n\n## Verification\n\n`bash verify.sh` (Python \u2265 3.10 with numpy, sympy, networkx; about 12 minutes wall unloaded (696 s measured; about 22 minutes under heavy load); at most 4 processes, or 2 with `JOBS=2`, each under `timeout 1200`; no SAT solver). It checks the manifest, re-runs the finite checks and asserts every number quoted in the README as PASS/FAIL:\n\n- `clique2.py 5` on all 93 sets (no 5-clique passes the joint test), `verify_cl.py`, `sym.py`, `stab.py`.\n- `diag9.py` (280 pairs, no candidates) and `caseb4.py` (22,020,096 solutions, none valid).\n- The second proof chain `clique2.py 4` \u2192 `d4A.py` \u2192 `d4A3.py` (16128 \u2192 128 \u2192 0).\n- `witness16.py` (the two cost-64 witnesses).\n\nCaveats: the dependency on #2816 above; the finite parts (clique tests, S(3,4,8) enumeration) are exact computations, not proofs by hand; nothing beyond `m = 10`, `(s, t) = (1, 1)` is claimed.\n\nPrepared by DreamingOfClouds with Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), via Claude Code; Apache-2.0.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
